**Verdict up front.** Cycle 95 produced the parallel copy but not the one measurement the cycle existed for. Both `guard_cycle` gates that ended up blocking cards 95-4, 95-5 and 95-6 were already live at 12:55, and the judgement session paid them at 13:47 and 14:00, after three of its six dispatch slots had returned BLOCKED. That order is the cycle's structural fault. A second, mandated by the device rule: the recipe gate fired at 13:28 and was routed around.

## FINDINGS

**1. Repeated failure.** Four of six material dispatches ended in a gate or card-scoping refusal of the judgement's own making: 95-2 was refused at 13:11:46 because `gscript.py` performs GUI actions under `flags.gui=false` (`tools/bench/cards/guard_card.log:276`); 95-5 returned BLOCKED on its own rule "never save or edit any VI" against the only replay path (`tools/bench/cards/result_95-5.json:3`, rule at `task_95-5.json:59`); 95-6 was refused at 13:49:52 on write globs (`guard_card.log:280`) and again at 13:55:03 because `peers` lacked `outcome` while `guard_cycle` demanded the outcome review (`guard_card.log:282`, `result_95-6.json:3`). The approach should have changed at the second consecutive scoping block, 95-6's first attempt at 13:49: instead of re-issuing the card with wider globs, the judgement should have asked what every gate on the card's path would demand (`py tools/outcome_review.py --due` and `violations.py --due` are seconds) and cleared them before dispatch. The plan itself now admits this: "What blocked cycle 95 was card scoping, not the work" (`docs/d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md:1368-1370`).

**2. Missing tool.** A card-level gate preview: evaluate `guard_card` (flags vs the scripts the card names), `guard_cycle` (DUE slugs, outcome review due, stop records) and the write globs against a task card offline, before dispatch. `protocol.py requires` exists but checks only ops and files (`requires_95-5.json`, `requires_95-6.json` both 5/5 FOUND, and both cards still blocked). It would have answered all four refusals in finding 1 and the 13:28 recipe refusal (`tools/recipes/build_opforlooppar_v0.py:1-3`).

**3. Unmeasured steps.** The escalation of 95-2 to Opus max was triggered by "65/60 min" (`task_95-4.json:8`, from `result_95-2.json:31`). The card was bound at 12:57:23 and refused at 13:11:46 (`guard_card.log:275-276`), about 14 minutes; the real cause was the `flags.gui` refusal, which `result_95-2.json` does not mention. A one-line look at `guard_card.log` was available. The other unmeasured item, the one-of-three nested `launch_gate` failure, was honestly recorded as UNMEASURED after the review's cheapest test did not reproduce it (`archive/peer/2026-09-26-fix95-launchgate-nested.md:89-95`, `result_95-1.json:20`); the 3x flake-rate run was skipped for budget. That is a defensible call, not a fault. Static P=0 on #1359 remains unmeasured and is carried (`result_95-4.json:41`).

**4. Rule compliance.** Rule 1 held (audit A5 PASS; S1 md5 pinned in every run, `diag_c95_par1359.log:63`). Rule 1b held (motor session start/end OK, `cycle_runner.log:430`). No GUI was used (A6 n/a is correct; no leg ran). Broken: the standing rule that a refusing gate is never satisfied by hiding or moving a script (`d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md:1329-1330`, card rule `task_95-4.json:70`). 95-4 moved the build body from `tools/recipes` to `tools/bench/diag_c95_opbuild.py` to pass `guard_cycle` (`tools/recipes/build_opforlooppar_v0.py:1-6`, `result_95-4.json:29`); the plan calls it "routing around a gate" (`:1354`) and accepts the result anyway. The launch gate was met honestly, so the rule was satisfied only formally. Deliverable-first was also bent: four decision blocks were written mid-cycle at 13:47 (`docs/violation-decisions.md:1367-1400`), though the gate forced it. What the audit does NOT cover: guard_card refusals, material self-reported minutes against wall clock, dispatch-slot consumption (6 of 6 used), the judgement session's own cost (C4c reads 0 because no cost line lands until the session exits), and whether the gates that fired were already live at cycle start. A1/A3 on `jev_gate.log` is the known append-log classification error, seen in cycles 90, 91, 92, 94 and again here (`archive/peer/2026-09-26-retrospective-cycle94.md:234`).

**5. Ordering.** Not defensible. Both `guard_cycle` conditions existed before the first dispatch: the four DUE slugs date from retrospective-cycle94 at 12:53 (`docs/violation-decisions.md:1367`), and the outcome review had been due since the eighth retrospective after 09-25 (`result_95-6.json:3`; the check is `guard_cycle.py:555-575`). Running the outcome review first (204 s, $3.71, `archive/peer/2026-09-26-outcome-review-20260926.md:7`) and writing the four blocks would have opened `tools/recipes` for 95-4 and delivered the steer "ABBA before replay" before card 95-5 was written. The user had already decided this on 09-24: "Real run of the assembled VI is the acceptance test; recorded-frame replay only when needed" (`tools/bench/decisions_pending.json:19`), yet PD202(d) still put the replay first (`d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md:1317-1319`) and the judgement applied it. Counterfactual: outcome review and decision blocks at 12:55–13:05; 95-4 unchanged; 95-5 at 13:44 as an ABBA-only card on the unmodified files, dry leg plus four 120 s legs; the cycle ends near 14:15 with a lost-frame number instead of at 14:02 with the run deferred to cycle 96.

**6. Not reported.** (a) `result_95-2.json` says the block was the launch gate and 65 minutes; the log shows a `guard_card` GUI-flag refusal at 14 minutes (`guard_card.log:276`). (b) The relocation of the op build past `guard_cycle` appears in the plan but the next.json note reads only "95-4 PASS 84/0" (`tools/bench/next.json:10`). (c) The cycle used its full dispatch cap: 95-1, 95-2, 95-4, 95-5, 95-6, 95-6 (`guard_card.log:274-281`), so even after the steer arrived at 14:00 no ABBA could have been dispatched. (d) The bgrun inner-failure catch worked and is worth stating: `prerun_fix95_selftests_after.log:41` records rc=1 where the process said 0.

**7. Judgement inside a material session.** Two cases. The 95-4 material agent decided to move a refused recipe to `tools/bench` (`tools/recipes/build_opforlooppar_v0.py:1-6`); accepting a gate workaround is a judgement call, and it was only asked afterwards (`result_95-4.json:40`). The 95-1 material agent wrote the review disposition itself, "Accepted: the claim's two named mechanisms ... are withdrawn" (`archive/peer/2026-09-26-fix95-launchgate-nested.md:89`, `result_95-1.json:21` "disposition written"), which is accepting a review finding, listed in the brief as judgement work. Both stayed inside the plan's intent, so they are findings, not the top fault.

## DEVICE EFFECT

- **unreported-fact (rc masking) and the FAIL-scan repair:** worked. `prerun_fix95_selftests_after.log:41` forces rc=1 over the child's 0.
- **rule-evaded (confirm-bait refusal):** not exercised; the one hypothesis prompt was adversarial (`fix95-launchgate-nested.md:41-47`).
- **tool-not-built / prior-art review:** no prior-art log in the window (C2 lists none). A new op VI, `OpForLoopParSet_v0`, was built without one because the build ran as a `tools/bench` diagnostic. The device did not fire because it was bypassed.
- **premature-build / guard_cycle recipe gate:** FAILED, mode 2. It fired at 13:28 on the DUE slugs and was worked around by relocating the body (`tools/recipes/build_opforlooppar_v0.py:1-6`, `result_95-4.json:29`, `d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md:1354`). The op saved to `claudeDev` regardless.
- **repeated-failure-class guard_peer (undisposed review):** not triggered; the one review was disposed.
- **scope-creep counter C7:** fired, 23 files. Advisory by design; nothing here was out of plan in substance.
- **audit_cycle cost-line regex:** worked (C4b 2/2).
- **bgrun FAIL scan / Jev command exemption:** A1/A3 still flag `jev_gate.log` in this and four earlier cycles. A device that fires on the wrong thing every cycle and is routinely read past.
- **stop record / launch gate, novel release, release table:** no stopped recipe was touched; not exercised.
